WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.420
-So, let's just start off, you know, you've often&nbsp;
said that your mission is to eliminate small talk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.560
So, I'm wondering how that became important to&nbsp;
you and what impact you think it's going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.860
to eradicate small talk. -Yeah, before anybody gets&nbsp;
too angry. I'll clarify that for me, small talk has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:23.100
nothing to do with the content of a conversation.&nbsp;
It has to do with the process. And so, I have had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.940
a conversation about the weather that I would&nbsp;
absolutely put in the category of conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:32.400
that mattered deeply. It happened to be with a man&nbsp;
named Eugene Claudio who is a meteorologist and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.580
we were studying seasonal affective disorder. But&nbsp;
the idea is like, you can talk about the weather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:42.060
if the process, if you're really curious about&nbsp;
it, if it matters, Etc. And so, I got clear a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:48.420
years ago on this kayak camping retreat in the&nbsp;
middle of nowhere Maine and the intention was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:54.600
to kind of disconnect from the world, reconnect&nbsp;
with yourself and some higher purpose if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.240
believe there even is one, and very much to my&nbsp;
surprise on that trip. I got really crystal clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.220
language that was Chad you exist to gently&nbsp;
eradicate small talk and create conversations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:12.540
that matter. And after that, all these like little&nbsp;
flashes of notable historical figures showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:21.780
up for me. And so, can you imagine like Buddha or&nbsp;
Gandhi or Rosa Parks or Jesus or anybody else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:27.240
who like has made history in some way walking&nbsp;
up to you and saying how was your weekend? I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.280
sure they did it, right? I'm totally, I'm sure&nbsp;
that all of them at some point asked a question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:38.040
like that, but I think the people in my life who&nbsp;
like really change things and shift things have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:43.500
a way of cutting to the heart of what matters.&nbsp;
And so, you know, a mentor of mine Jeff, 15&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.760
minutes before I got married walked straight up&nbsp;
to me no niceties, no hellos, put his hand on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.240
shoulder and just said, are you present? Because&nbsp;
you really don't want to miss your own wedding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.680
And 10 years ago, I'm really glad he did that&nbsp;
because I was actually there. And so, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.780
a couple to answer your question like&nbsp;
specifically and concretely, I think we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:07.380
probably double the amount of good stuff that&nbsp;
happens in the world if we cut out like idle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:13.560
chit-chat just because. Now, I still think you can&nbsp;
ask, how are you? What do you do? Where are you from?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:19.200
All these questions and have it matter. But I think&nbsp;
it's also interesting to note like we're we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:26.640
in such a routine with the questions we ask that&nbsp;
just by disrupting those questions a little bit, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.000
